 *     <div class="flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-muted text-muted-foreground font-semibold min-h-0">5</div>
 *     <div class="flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-muted text-muted-foreground font-semibold min-h-0">6</div>
 *   </CarouselSlide>
 * </Carousel>
 *
 * ## Navigation and accessibility
 *
 * Use the previous and next buttons, drag with a mouse, or swipe with touch. Focus the slide viewport to use Left/Right (horizontal), Up/Down (vertical), Home, or End. Data-slot manages slide semantics, inactive content, disabled controls, and reduced-motion scrolling. Give each carousel an accessible name with `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`.
 *
 * ## Multiple cards
 *
 * Each `CarouselSlide` is one full viewport page and can contain any number of elements. With `variant="multiple"`, the default layout uses two columns horizontally or two rows vertically, as shown in the examples. This is a layout default, not a limit on slide content; customize the slide layout for other card counts or responsive arrangements. Navigation advances one page at a time, keeping all content in the active slide accessible. A counter on this variant counts pages.
 *
 * ## Sizing and migration
 *
 * Use `class` for the carousel's outer width and `contentClass` for the viewport. Vertical carousels need an explicit viewport height. Slides fill the viewport; keep card dimensions inside the slide. The former `axis="y"` spelling remains an alias for `orientation="vertical"`. Scroll spacing and height classes previously placed on the root should move to `contentClass`. The old `align` prop is replaced by full-page start snapping so buttons, gestures, and keyboard navigation share the same positions.
 *
 * ## Events
 *
 * Listen for `carousel:change` on the root to read `event.detail.index`. Dispatch `new CustomEvent("carousel:set", { detail: { index: 2 } })` on the root to select a page, or use `{ action: "next" }` / `{ action: "prev" }` to navigate.
